# SYNTHESISED to exercise the stall annotation over a wait that is split
# by handovers inside the lane doing the blocking. One short google call
# runs early and google never runs again; four aws calls then hand over to
# each other back to back until the log ends. google's lane is therefore
# idle continuously from 3.3s to 20.0s -- ONE 16.7-second wait -- even
# though four different aws spans block it in turn, and the annotation
# must report it as one wait of that length rather than as the four
# fragments the sweep sees. It also carries the window before any provider
# call at all: 3.0s of capture time in which Terraform core was starting
# up, which is a wait of a different kind and is worded as one.
#
# Boundary times are round multiples of 100ms so formatMs's one-decimal
# rendering has nothing to round. The 3.0s-3.1s sliver before the google
# call opens is below the annotation's 1s/5% threshold and is dropped,
# which is why the reported wait starts at 3.3s and not at 3.0s.
#
# Lanes are packed per provider, so aws's four calls share one lane and
# google's single call has its own: the annotation names "aws/1", and
# providers are concatenated in ascending label order, so aws is the first
# row and google the second.
#
# The line shape is testdata/two-providers.log's "Received downstream
# response" verbatim. The first line is not itself a response: it anchors
# the scanner's base clock a few seconds before the first real span, so
# that span's start is not clamped to zero by ReportedBuilder (see the
# doc comment on span.Span). Values are invented; no line here is from a
# real run.
